LONE SURVIVOR is like a bit-sized 2D Silent Hill with a very David Lynch style (Twin Peaks, Eraserhead, Blue Velvet) twist to it. If you're into horror games like Silent Hill, Alan Wake, Deadly Premonition, or the horror RPG classic Sweet Home, or just weird, bizarre, freaky, psychological stuff, definitely check this out. It's one of my favorites of 2012, and I was really glad when I saw it as part of the Humble Indie Bundle.
There are multiple endings (Red, Green, Blue) depending on which pill you take, and your action choices throughout the game. Let's see which one you get.. In the meantime, I'll be looking forward to Jasper Byrne's next game which is called NEW GAME+.
// Roth Sothy

TL;DR: this game is totally worth to try and most likely you will have fun, and it's actually free.
Let me start with a cons list:
* detalisation and textures quality is a little bit lacking (well, at least in a full HD) and clearly not as flashy as Tera or Blade & Soul (well, we have to take real game age and art style into consideration here);
* major fractions is a bit disbalanced in amout of players ('cause everyone want to have an Aurin girl in their characters list);
* it's f2p, so if you pay you get more... kinda, it isn't pay2win (so, it isn't really an issue), some people just don't like f2p even though it isn't that bad when it's done right (more about that below);
* standard interface is lacking in functionality and occasionally glitches (rarely for me), but it's possible to fix with addons to some degree (so, it isn't really an issue too);
* as I know there are a few missable achievements which is kinda strange for an MMO... but, seriously, if you don't care what NPCs telling you then it's your own fault;
* at least a few people wasn't able to run this game for some reason, but it's something you will only know if you try (most likely it will work);
* well, I can't remember anything else to list here (maybe just haven't encountered yet).
So, with that list of cons we can move to pros list:
* this game reminds me World of Warcraft both in art style and mechanics, but with free targeting, more action and sci-fi setting... and as hard as before WoW became a world for casual players, so modern WoW players will say dungeons here are less forgiving... well, you will figure that out on your own when the final boss in the very first dungeon will wipe your entire party if you will fail to kill him fast enough;
* you can totally play it with mouse-look targeting enabled all the time (+ most of your skills are AoE, so it's hard to miss, but you have to enable mouse-look in the options - it isn't default setting);
* similar to WoW here is a wide variety of quest objectives including stuff like throwing eggs at the enemies to make huge birds attack them to death or blowing up big portion of a map while cinematically driving through explosions;
* there are multiple different "dungeon" types including missions in the outer space (with absolutely awesome intros and outros in old black-and-white sci-fi movies art style), actual dugeons and "simulations" in the "virtual environment" (Exibit would be glag to comment on this) where you can freely go in any direction and vote for the quest to pick with yout party;
* Aurin girls are so nice to look at ^_^;
* locations are freaking huge and filled with content all over the top, it will be really hard to visit every nook and cranny, add tons of challenges and secondary job missions to that and you have hours and hours of pure fun;
* the game will grant you a huge portion of the land for free (separate instance from the wold) where you can build your house and some extra stuff (majority of the upgrades and decors either sold for the in-game currency and rather cheap or available via a drop, some stuff is given for finishing quests, there is a limit on the number of decor in the house placed at the same time, but you have to buy quite a lot of stuff to actually reach it and you can increase it with omnibits if you really think it's not enough... most likely you will hit it if you decide to build your own house from the basic parts and without actual interior instance, all bought houses have a separate interior with separate limit, but lack in ability to peek inside or outside through a window).
A bit more on a F2P in this game: it's that case when the game doesn't try to really discriminate you if you are not paying. Of courese you can buy some stuff in the store, but it doesn't give you any advantages besides being instant. For example, you can't buy speed bonus for your mount for the in-game money before you reach a certain level and it will worth significant amount of gold (at that level), but you can buy it for money in the store, but you totally can affort these speed upgrades later on. Also, you will gain OmniBits (special in-game currency) for finishing various instances and buy items in the store for this currency, so yes, you can buy almost anything from the store for the omnibits. The only issue is a weekly limit on the numbet of omnibits you can get (subscription page says it's 120 per week), however all the important stuff doesn't worth too much, so you can totally afford it in a 1-3 weeks. I'm not sure why, but I totally went over the limit and still got extra omnibits, maybe it isn't really 120 per week or some other mechanic was involved. It's totally worth to subscribe, though, since you will get more experience, more chances on the loot in the dungeons (extra chest and 1 free key for these chests each day), free hoverboard (and that is really cool type of "mounts"), higher omnibit weekly limit and more omnibits per drop (so, you can buy a lot of extra stuff without throwing any extra money), but all that won't really affect your power. You just will get to the higher levels faster, unlock limitations faster and will have a bit more opportunities like more drop chances, more slots on the auction or priority in the queues and that's all about it. You won't get any extra content, unavailable to he free players, or OP items.
BTW, regarding the hoverboard: you can buy one later on for the in-game currency (not for omnibits, but for renowns) or go grab that E3 Humble Bundle for $1 while it lasts and get a really odd one. There are no difference in the mounts speed, so it's ok. The only difference is if you have a rider licenses or not. Also, at the low levels you can rent a ground mount really cheap.
So, from what I see right now I can totally recommend this game. There are tremendous amount of content to play throught and have fun with, and it's free and if you get bored with your current fraction/class/job for some reason, then create another character in the opposite fraction with different class and job and have fun with it. There are tons of fraction-specific content! The only issue is lack of Aurin on the Dominion side ("thanks" to Chua for this, they did really unforgivable thing to the entire Aurin race).

Can be made better, but it's still enjoyable...
Why i like it:
* minesweeper! {
}
*
with some interesting twists. That get revealed while progressing through, not all at once. :-bd {
}
* windowed mode. :-) {
}
* steam achievements. {
}
* small storage footprint. {
}
Why i am tempted to dislike it at times:
* Doesn't auto-finish when "0 remaining"... {
}
* No left+right click on a "completed" hint to reveal remaining
cells that are obviously empty. Guess i'm too used to this esoteric mechanic from the Windows Minesweeper. :-)
- I can't detect any highlight (or shadow) of the not-yet-perfected levels! Do i have to go through all of them to get the final achievement?! :-s {
}
* No volume control for the music: it's just 100% or 0%. At least i can mute it.
* No randomization. As a programmer myself, i would expect "generate board and try to solve" is not that difficult to include. Though, TBF, Hexcells Infinite does contain a random puzzle generator.
- No "level title" to tell me which level i'm currently solving. I usually minimize (or alt+tab out of) the game, and don't remember this bit of detail when i come back to it... Also, when going to the next level, i usually lose track. Guess exiting to menu to check which level is the first one unsolved is doable... But it's annoying. {
}
* The "column-counter" hints can initially be easily misinterpreted to be "neighbour-counter" hints that are just placed on an invisible tile... Which might lead to mistakes! Eh, i guess it just takes a bit to get used to them. {
}
*
Oh, so i have to manually do it... AND there's a guideline too?! Why is this mentioned so late after introducing the "outside number" hints?? :-< {
}
* Cannot save level state... {
}
* Inconsistent mechanic of the "-X-" hints, when they're inside a cell or outside the board... {
}
- No "Exit" button. Most people don't really need it, but it still would've been nice to have.
Update 2017-07-01: three of the dislikes i stated above are now somewhat addressed. Maybe they were from the start and i missed them, or maybe they were implemented in the mean time. But i still believe the level number (why is this NOT the x-y combo from the menu, but a counter not shown anywhere else?!), the Quit button, and the difference between perfected and completed-with-mistakes levels are WAY too faint to be really noticeable. :-s

In this game there are serious problems with bugs and balance, which have long been written on the forum. Instead of dealing with patches, developers release useless updates that only exacerbate the situation. Now the roles of the characters are distorted, the heal can be a damage, the tank can be flank, the flank is quite capable of giving more damage than the dmg. Here, the losses in the ranking due to the crash twice in a row or the selection of rivals for several titles higher in the order of things. Enumerate the problems can be long, there are a whole bunch of them in this game and no one is engaged in them. Finally, it is worth mentioning the latest update, which turns the Paladins into a P2W project. Thus, if you want your character is not inferior to others, you need to spend a lot of gold to level up cards only 1 character and much more to improve the card level for the rest of the characters for which you want to play. The developers wanted to make the characters more accessible to the game, but it turned out exactly the opposite.
Summarizing, you can identify several arguments why you should not play:
-Problems with Balance;
-Problems with Bugs;
-Developers neglect existing problems and only exacerbate them;
-Now its a Pay to Win game.
Why you should try this game:
-If you are ready to put up with all the shortcomings and can afford to spend a lot of time on the grind of gold, this game may well be an interesting experience for you. Paladins are some kind of alternative to an Overwatch, perhaps even more interesting.
But now its a laggy-buggy trash :) So do not waste your time and play in something really worthwhile. Maybe someday the developers still take the game seriously, but not now.

